# braces: stack-exhaustion denial of service through deeply nested patterns.
# Affected range <=3.0.3, and 3.0.3 is the newest version published, so there is
# no fix to take — this is the finding that made this file necessary.
#
# WHY A BUMP CANNOT CLEAR IT, checked rather than assumed. `npm view braces
# versions` ends at 3.0.3. npm offers two "fixes" and both are major downgrades
# of direct dependencies: eslint-config-next 16.3.6 -> 14.2.35 and
# shadcn ^4.21.0 -> 1.0.0. eslint-config-next 14 against next 16.3.6 would trade
# an audit failure for a lint failure, which is not a fix. Dropping one
# dependency does not work either: braces is reached through two independent
# paths, eslint-config-next -> @next/eslint-plugin-next -> fast-glob ->
# micromatch and shadcn -> fast-glob, so clearing it by subtraction means giving
# up both the Next lint config and the component CLI.
#
# WHY IT DOES NOT APPLY. Three things have to hold, and all three do.
#
# It is build-time only. braces is not in the shipped tree at all — the runtime
# gate (`npm audit --omit=dev`) reports zero vulnerabilities, so nothing here
# reaches a user of the deployed app. That is why this entry carries no `runtime`
# marker, and the gate will reject it if that ever changes.
#
# The impact is availability only. CVSS 3.1 7.5
# AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H — no confidentiality, no integrity. The
# failure mode is the globbing process exhausting its stack and dying, and the
# processes doing the globbing are `eslint` and the shadcn CLI. A crashed lint
# run is a red check, not a breach.
#
# The patterns are ours. The advisory needs a deeply nested brace *pattern*, and
# the patterns reaching braces here are the glob patterns in eslint.config.mjs
# and components.json plus shadcn's own internal file globs. They are committed
# to this repository, not read from user input, a request, or the database. There
# is no path by which an outside party supplies one.
#
# WHAT UNBLOCKS REMOVING IT. A braces release above 3.0.3, or
# @next/eslint-plugin-next and shadcn moving off fast-glob. Neither looks
# imminent: braces' newest publish is 3.0.3 and fast-glob's is 3.3.3 from
# 2025-01-05, with nothing since. Neither is formally deprecated on npm, so this
# is a quiet dependency rather than an abandoned one — which is the weaker
# claim, and the one actually checkable. Re-check with
# `cd app && npm view braces versions` and `npm audit --audit-level=high`.
#
# THE DATE IS SIX WEEKS OUT, AND IT IS NOT WAITING ON A REBUILD. The .trivyignore
# entries use short dates because an upstream base rebuild is a scheduled,
# expected event. This one is waiting on an upstream release that may never
# arrive, so the date is not a countdown to a fix — it is a prompt to re-ask a
# different question: whether a glob stack neither of whose links has shipped in
# a year is still worth carrying for a lint config and a component CLI. If this
# entry reaches a
# second extension, that is the signal the .trivyignore header gives for its own
# entries, and the answer is to change the dependency rather than the date.
